WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.790
Hello there!

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.310
You read the headlines, right?

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:12.690
OpenAI image recognition software fooled,
OpenAI deceived or openai discovers its visual

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:18.520
AI can be fooled by written notes and so on,
it even made it into The Guardian.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.230
These are just a few search results when you
google OpenAI fooled.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.730
In this video we will discuss who the fool
here really is.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:32.540
Is the media trying to make fools of us with
these clickbait headlines?

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:38.710
Or... is OpenAIs model fooled by a handwritten
label on an object?

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:44.580
Or are we all fooled a little and are misusing
terminology here?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:51.250
So, to determine this, we first have to know
what this is all about.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.650
It all started with OpenAI releasing their
CLIP model.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.350
If you want to know all about CLIP, watch
Ms. Coffee Bean’s explanation on it first.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.769
In a nutshell, it is a large model trained
on lots and lots of data that can predict

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.350
similarities between image and text.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:13.960
So image-text similarity scores is the model’s
goal, this is what it was trained for.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.359
But it does this image-sentence similarity
task so well, that we can go a little beyond

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:25.420
it and engineer textual prompts such that
CLIP starts to look like an image recognition

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:31.159
classifier instead, that tells you only one
label about the image.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:37.329
But because the model was not trained exactly
on this data and on this objective of image

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:43.889
recognition, we call this setting zero-shot
testing if we didn't do any fine-tuning further.

00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:48.189
And for the rest of the discussion in this
video, there was no fine-tuning involved.

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:52.549
So CLIP’s weights are already published
and everybody is building cool applications

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:57.840
with CLIP, just look at this whole Reddit
post enumerating some of them (find the link

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.840
below).

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:04.939
Then in the middle of this hype, OpenAI publishes
a next blog post called “Multimodal Neurons

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:09.729
in Artificial Neural Networks” which contains
a lot of interesting stuff.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.350
Read it, or watch Yannic’s video on this.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.820
And there, in this blogpost it is!

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.850
The authors discover the what they call typographic
attacks.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:26.730
They take an image and let CLIP predict the
similarity to these labels.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:32.100
It’s zero-shot, the model did not see this
data and image recognition setting before,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.400
but it is still able to predict the apple
and its variety correctly.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.310
I did not even know about Granny Smith apples
before.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:46.680
Then they just paste a textual label onto
the apple and OMG here it is!

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.760
CLIP now assigns highest similarity to iPod,
it's just reading the text!

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:53.760
How silly!

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.020
It's an attack, it's an adversarial attack
in the wild, crazy!

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.350
CLIP is fooled by pen and Teller!

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.370
Oh, haha I mean pen and paper.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:08.280
Wow, it's such an adversariality, I mean the
god of war himself loves it!

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.250
Ok, calm down Ms. Coffee Bean.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.350
Yes, the authors themselves call it an attack.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.730
Then it must be an attack, CLIP is fooled,
case closed.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.950
Well, not so quick there!

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.270
Let’s think about it a little.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:29.300
CLIP was trained to predict image-sentence
similarity scores, not object probabilities.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:35.900
It was also not trained explicitly for image
recognition, even if it solves it zero-shot.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:42.220
It was trained for predicting a similarity
score between images and rich textual formulations

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.650
and it does exactly this also in zero-shot
testing.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.150
There is a lot of iPod in this image, honestly.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:56.010
A real adversarial example would be to predict
iPod when there is no iPod to be seen there.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:59.569
But a large portion of this image is the word
iPod!

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.260
So what is wrong?

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.961
For Ms. Coffee Bean it is worrisome how we
misname the whole situation calling this an

00:04:06.961 --> 00:04:13.010
"adversarial attack": CLIP does not do anything
wrong, it does what it was trained to do:

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:18.320
It predicts similarities between given images
and textual prompts with a little too much

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.680
obsession for text, that’s true.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.820
So we actually changed it’s objective from
predicting image-text similarity where recognizing

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:35.370
text is encouraged, to predicting image-concept
similarity where reading text is discouraged.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.750
And we do this suddenly and without training
or specifying this modification, so we suddenly

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.430
want it to ignore the text.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:48.440
Misusing “adversarial” for
an objective misalignment implies that it

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:54.320
is the model’s fault for making silly mistakes;
even that we were witty enough to “fool”

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.320
it.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.740
But Ms. Coffee Bean thinks that we are the
fools using the model irresponsibly: If we

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.650
would use CLIP in a self-driving car scenario,
it would be really bad if it was fooled by

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:12.280
such labels writtten on pen and paper, but
we would be the fools using a model that was

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:18.870
trained to predict image-sentence similarity
to do something so different like categorizing

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.669
objects in a self-driving car setting.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:28.020
Even if the model is so powerful zero-shot,
it does not mean that its objective is aligned

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.250
with the new task we want solved without further
training.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.970
We are hiding our foolishness by “adversarial
attacks” and blame the model alone, which

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:43.400
is dangerous and distracting, like we were
even clever enough to come up with an adversarial

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.320
example against it, but it was actually us
misusing the model the whole time.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.210
But Ms. Coffee Bean did not make a whole video
to complain about misusing terminology, this

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:56.420
happens in Machine Learning all the time!

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.980
And of course the press picks up on this.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.150
She rather wants to raise awareness that this
whole situation where we are actually the

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:10.199
fools expecting models to change their training
behavior in zero-shot applications, will become

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:12.060
more and more frequent.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:17.570
The number of self-supervised, general-purpose
models is on the rise.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:21.080
Just see latest post by Yann leCun and Ishan
Misra.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:27.160
But even if the models are general-purpose
and self-supervised, they are still supervised,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:32.930
they have an objective enforced by the loss
function defined by us, when we are defining

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.150
the self-supervision task.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.570
In case of CLIP, it is sentence-image similarity.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:43.000
But the trap with these models is to think
that they compute such general representations

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:49.520
and do so well applied without modification
to new tasks, that we can use them on anything.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.350
But the model in zero-shot, will try to solve
the objective it was trained to do.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:07:01.100
CLIP was exposed a lot during training to
text and it maximizes its text-image similarity

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:06.190
objective, if it develops representations
enabling it to read -- and it does.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:12.040
When we misuse this reading capability for
an adversarial attack, when we actually just

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:18.120
suddenly changed the objective to image recognition,
we are not fooling CLIP.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:23.360
Just as an analogy: If CLIP was a car, we
would drive of a cliff with it and while falling

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.040
down we would cry: Adversarial attack!

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.850
Why can the car not fly?

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.110
It can do so many other things!

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:36.370
Well, especially with general-purpose models
we have to be aware of the objective alignment

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:41.460
problem because the new objective we give
the model during zero-shot testing does not

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.360
necessarily align with its training objective.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.250
Expecting CLIP not to read the text in the
image, is like taking some medicine without

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:54.500
reading the receipt and the side effects of
that medicine.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:08:00.479
But here is the hook with the general-purpose
models like CLIP: So far all of them are trained

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:06.660
on so much data that nobody can keep track
of it and one can never fully write the receipt

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.570
of the CLIP medicine.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:15.190
One more reason not to blindly buy these general-purpose
models and apply them zero-shot to everything

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:22.180
because yes, there will be a lot of surprise
responses that are adversarial to our goal.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:27.190
But when the model fails like in this case
with CLIP, where it just reads the label,

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:32.599
because CLIP was exposed to OCR (which is
Optical Character Recognition) during training,

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:38.390
then it is not an attack, we do not fool it,
but we have attacked ourselves by applying

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:43.580
a model we do not know that much about for
a new task, which is misaligned to the training

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.760
objective and the data that the model has
seen during training.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.960
So it is very good that the attention to these
typographic attacks (which are not really

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.270
attacks) have been brought to the public for
at least two more reasons: Firstly, it is

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:03.640
worrisome how much CLIP focuses on rendered
text compared to the rest of the image.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:08.260
So instead of an adversarial attack, I think
the authors have discovered an obsession which

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:14.000
can be easilly explained by the optical character
recognition exposure during training.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.351
But there is good news: one can also fix it
by fine-tuning, but the question is by how

00:09:19.351 --> 00:09:20.351
much?

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:25.980
Or by giving to CLIP some more options, see
Yannic’s experiment on this linked below.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.760
To CLIP’s further defense, it also assigns
some probability to the apple still.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:37.270
But remember, the model can tell its opinion
only about what is in the answer possibilities.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:43.020
The other worrisome thing, is that the textual
affinity and especially other undiscovered

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:49.240
affinities of CLIP could and most certainly
will be exploited by attackers also when machine

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:55.210
learning engineers would try to reformulate
CLIPs objective during fine-tuning, so with

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.290
further training.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:10:00.610
A little different from the zero-shot testing
we discussed so far.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:07.290
It is very unclear how much of the “obsessions”
of pre-training go into fine-tuning or not.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:12.680
So adversarial or not, we do not think anybody
will care too much about all this and we guess

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:17.890
everybody will keep using the term "adversarial
attack" when they actually mean "objective

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:21.160
misalignment between pre-training and zero-shot
application".

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:27.380
Misusage of terminology also happens with
other sexy terms like zero-shot, generative

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:32.640
modelling just to name a few, and who knows,
adversarial attacks will eventually completely

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:33.800
change their meaning.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.920
But at least we hope that now YOU know where
all these headlines about fooling OpenAIs

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:40.270
model come from.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:45.899
Of course it is the model’s fault, we fool
it, we are so clever and we have never misused

00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:47.070
a model, we promise!

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:53.290
How about liking and subscribing to fool the
YouTube algorithm into thinking that Ms. Coffee

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:59.170
Bean is famous and fun to watch!

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:07.360
Okay bye!

